# Political Comms integration rules

You are integrating with Political Comms, the direct-to-carrier political texting platform.

## API basics
- Base URL: https://api.politicalcomms.com/v1
- Auth: X-API-Key header. Keys are prefixed pc_live_ and are generated by a human in the dashboard (app.politicalcomms.com, Admin → API). Keys are shown once.
- Official SDKs: @political-comms/sdk (npm, TypeScript), political-comms (PyPI), @political-comms/cli, and @political-comms/mcp (MCP server). Direct HTTP against the spec works equally well.
- Source of truth for endpoints: https://docs.politicalcomms.com/api-reference/openapi.json
- Docs: https://docs.politicalcomms.com/api-reference/introduction
- Full machine-readable reference: https://politicalcomms.com/llms-full.txt

## Rules
- Always consult the OpenAPI spec before writing a request. Never fabricate endpoints, parameters, or response fields.
- Read API keys from environment variables or a secret manager. Never hardcode keys, never commit them, never print them in logs or output.
- Respect the rate limit: 600 requests per minute per key, the same for every scope: bursts of up to 600 at once, refilling at 10 per second. Read the X-RateLimit-* response headers and back off before the ceiling.
- Errors are structured JSON: {success, error, code, statusCode}. Branch on code and statusCode, not on the error string. Do not retry 4xx with the same payload. Retry 5xx with exponential backoff.
- Set an Idempotency-Key header (a UUID) on write requests you might retry, especially POST /projects/{id}/schedule.
- Sending is two calls: POST /projects (organization_id, name, protocol, message_text, brand_id + campaign_id on the 10dlc channel, phone_number_ids, contact_list_ids), then POST /projects/{id}/schedule (scheduled_at with UTC offset + scheduled_timezone). Validation is strict: unknown body properties are rejected with a 400.
- Carrier throughput: T-Mobile caps messages per day per brand for brands that have a T-Mobile daily limit on file (political brands have none today), resetting at midnight Pacific. At the cap, recipients on known other carriers keep sending; T-Mobile and unknown-carrier recipients wait, and when only those remain the project auto-pauses (`GET /projects/{id}` returns `status: paused`, `pause_reason: brand_daily_cap`, `auto_paused: true`). Run `POST /contact-lists/{id}/analyze` first and only T-Mobile recipients wait. Resume with `POST /projects/{id}/schedule` (it accepts paused projects): schedule it for the next day during sending hours (8 AM to 10 PM recipients' local time) with a morning `scheduled_at`, or resume now with `daily_cap_bypass: true` (over-cap T-Mobile may fail, still billed). Never resume at midnight. Every 10DLC campaign with an AT&T rate on file, political brands included, is paced per minute at that rate, counted in message parts (separate SMS and MMS rates; a two-part text counts twice); a campaign with no rate on file is not paced. AT&T pacing never slows other carriers and never pauses a project. Check before scheduling with `GET /campaigns/{id}/throughput` (`t_mobile.daily_cap`, `used_today`, `remaining_today`, `att.sms_tpm`, `att.mms_tpm`). The lanes are independent: `t_mobile` is null when the brand has no T-Mobile daily limit on file or it has not synced yet, `att` is null only when the campaign has no AT&T rate on file (`sms_tpm` and `mms_tpm` can each be null), and `carrier_metered` is true if either lane applies (a political brand with an AT&T rate returns true with `t_mobile` null and `att` populated; false with both null when neither applies). `GET /projects/{id}/throughput` gives `t_mobile.will_pause`, `t_mobile.estimated_send_days`, `att.estimated_minutes` (accounts for the parts in the project's text), `carrier_coverage`; with `daily_cap_bypass` on it reports `will_pause: false` and `estimated_send_days: 1`, results are cached up to 60 seconds, and a timed-out estimate returns 503 `CARRIER_ESTIMATE_TIMEOUT` (retry later). A null means not known right now, never zero (if `used_today` is null, `will_pause` is false). Poll for `paused`, not only `completed`: a `brand_daily_cap` pause is waiting for the next Pacific day, not stuck. Known `pause_reason` values: `brand_daily_cap` (schedule it for the next day's sending hours with a morning `scheduled_at`, or resume now with `daily_cap_bypass: true`), `quiet_hours` (paused at 10 PM recipients' local time; restart manually next morning), `carrier_block_rate`, `unregistered_campaign`, `provider_error`, `insufficient_funds_auto_recharge_failed`, `insufficient_funds_ancestor`, `shared_phone_revoked`, `phone_released`, `organization_deleted`, `manual` or a short free-text reason (up to 100 characters) set by a user; other values may appear. Every auto-pause needs a manual restart via `POST /projects/{id}/schedule`.
- Contact lists: `POST /contact-lists/{id}/analyze` runs line-type analysis and is billable per lookup, charged to the list's organization. Send an `Idempotency-Key`; `402 INSUFFICIENT_BALANCE` queues nothing; `202` returns `cost_cents` and `numbers_queued`; `200` with `analysis.status` `complete` (cost 0) means nothing was left to analyze; a run already in progress returns `202` with cost 0. It is async: poll `GET /contact-lists/{id}` until `analysis.status` is `complete`, or subscribe to the opt-in `contact_list.analyzed` webhook (payload `id`, `list_id`, `name`, `analysis`, `analyzed_url`). `GET /contact-lists/{id}` returns `downloads.original_url` and `downloads.analyzed_url` (null until complete); `GET /contact-lists/{id}/download?type=original|analyzed` streams the CSV (`type` required; analyzed adds Phone Type, Carrier Name, Is Mobile, Is Opted Out, City, State; `409 ANALYSIS_NOT_COMPLETE` before completion; `404` outside the key's scope). The download URLs need your `X-API-Key` and do not expire. `POST /projects`, project update, and `POST /projects/{id}/schedule` return `409 LIST_ANALYSIS_IN_PROGRESS` while a contact list on the project is being analyzed; `details.lists` names each list. The request changes nothing: wait for `analysis.status` `complete` (or `contact_list.analyzed`), then retry it.
- POST /projects, POST /projects/{id}/copy, and POST /v1/email/campaigns return 403 ONBOARDING_INCOMPLETE once an organization's 14-day account setup grace window has passed with its business profile or funding step still incomplete (details.missingSteps names what's outstanding, details.onboardingUrl is /onboarding). This never affects anything already running. Escalate to a human operator; do not retry.
- Some features require enablement by Political Comms: every write under /v1/email/* returns 403 ENTITLEMENT_REQUIRED if the organization lacks the entitlement (details.entitlement names the key: tendlc, toll_free, short_code, data_purchases, email, stripe_connect, phone_number_sharing, or sub_orgs). Escalate to a human operator to contact support; do not retry.
- Sending can be paused by Political Comms: POST /projects, POST /projects/{id}/schedule, POST /v1/conversations/{id}/messages, and POST /v1/email/campaigns/{id}/schedule return 409 SENDING_PAUSED with details.scope (organization or platform). Escalate to a human operator; do not retry.
- Replying to an inbound text: POST /conversations/{conversation_id}/messages with { "text": "..." } (SMS only, up to 1600 chars, same number as the thread; conversation_id comes from the message.replied webhook). 202 means queued; the outcome arrives on message.sent / message.delivered / message.failed. 409 CONTACT_OPTED_OUT and 409 SENDING_PAUSED are final, do not retry. 503 SEND_ENQUEUE_FAILED was not sent or charged, retry it; on other 5xx read GET /conversations/{id}/messages first. GET /conversations?updated_since= recovers inbounds missed by a down webhook endpoint (keyset paginated, poll at most once a minute).
- Webhooks: validate the HMAC-SHA256 signature in X-Webhook-Signature (format sha256=...) against the raw request body using a constant-time comparison before trusting any payload. Return 2xx quickly; non-2xx triggers retries, so make handlers idempotent.
- Webhook events: message.sent, message.delivered, message.failed, message.replied, link.clicked. Opt-in: contact_list.analyzed (list analysis finished). Email adds email.delivered, email.bounced, email.complained, email.unsubscribed, email.clicked (early access).
- Email (/v1/email: domains, senders, lists, list imports, suppressions, campaigns, templates) is EARLY ACCESS: every endpoint returns 403 EMAIL_EARLY_ACCESS until general availability. That is expected, not a bad key. Do not retry it. Once GA, every write additionally requires the email entitlement (403 ENTITLEMENT_REQUIRED, details.entitlement: "email"), and POST /v1/email/campaigns/{id}/schedule can return 409 SENDING_PAUSED. Email lists are keyset paginated ({data, has_more, next_cursor}), unlike the messaging endpoints; page until next_cursor is null and never parse a cursor. There is no inbox, no inbound email, no A/B testing, and no email.opened event. DNS is manual: publish the records the API returns. Removing list contacts unsubscribes them rather than deleting them. Suppression scope is org, identity, or domain and always applies; to hold people out of one campaign, put an ordinary email list in its suppression_list_ids. Read the blocked array from GET /v1/email/campaigns/{id} before scheduling, and never retry a resume that returns 409 EMAIL_CAMPAIGN_RESUME_REQUIRES_SUPPORT: escalate to a human.
- Email setup and paid workflows are dashboard-only, not API: registering sending domains and sender identities, renaming or deleting lists, paid validation, result exports, campaign pause/resume/test, and Lincoln drafting. POST /v1/email/lists/import imports a CSV you host over HTTPS in one call and returns 202, creating a NEW list from the file (no email_list_id: name defaults to the file name, email_domain_id scopes it to one sending domain); when it returns 400 VALIDATION_ERROR read details.headers and send a mapping naming the email column. Template writes return a lint object: errors there do not block the save but do block scheduling a campaign that uses the template.
- For documentation lookups, use the Political Comms MCP server (https://docs.politicalcomms.com/mcp, manifest at https://politicalcomms.com/.well-known/mcp.json) or fetch https://politicalcomms.com/llms.txt.
